each p[j] is read once per tile instead of once per i. That reduces memory traffic by factor TI. And it preserves summation order because for each i in tile, the additions from j=0..n-1 occur in that order (since we loop j ascending and add to force_i). Yes.\n\nSo algorithm:\nfor ii = 0; ii < n; ii += TI:\n  // process tile of i's [ii, min(ii+TI,n))\n  float fx[TI], fy[TI], fz[TI]; // but variable length? Use fixed max or allocate.\n  for t=0; t<TI && ii+t<n; ++t) { fx[t]=fy[t]=fz[t]=0; }\n  for j = 0; j < n; ++j:\n    float pjx=p[j].x, pjy=p[j].y, pjz=p[j].z;\n    for t=0; t<TI && ii+t<n; ++t):\n      int i = ii + t;\n      dx = pjx - p[i].x; ...\n      inv_dist3...\n      fx[t] += dx*inv_dist3; etc.\n  // after j loop, update velocities for tile:\n  for t=0...: p[ii+t].vx += dt*fx[t]; ...\n\nThis preserves order because for each i in tile, the additions to fx happen as j goes from 0..n-1 sequentially. Yes.\n\nWe can parallelize over ii tiles with OpenMP static scheduling; each thread handles a set of tiles (contiguous chunks). That gives both parallelism and cache reuse within tile.\n\nChoose TI maybe 8 or 16? For ARM, we want to fit p[i] block in registers/cache: TI*24 bytes plus force arrays. Let's pick TI=32 (768 bytes for positions) and j loop reads sequentially; good.\n\nWe also need restrict on pointer.\n\nThis is a solid memory-locality transformation with blocking and parallelization.\n\nLet's implement it. We'll use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) on the ii loop, but we must include <omp.h>? Not needed if using pragma only; compiler needs -fopenmp flag. The Makefile may have that. If not, compilation fails. To be safe, we could guard with _OPENMP macro: #ifdef _OPENMP ... #endif. But the controller compiles as is; if OpenMP not enabled, the pragmas are ignored (with warning maybe) and code still works sequentially. So it's fine to include them.\n\nWe'll also add restrict keyword in function signature.\n\nLet's write new body_force:\n\nvoid body_force(Body * restrict p, float dt, int n) {\n  const int TI = 32; // tile size for i\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int ii = 0; ii < n; ii += TI) {\n    int imax = ii + TI;\n    if (imax > n) imax = n;\n    float fx[TI], fy[TI], fz[TI];\n    // initialize\n    for (int t = 0; t < imax - ii; ++t) { fx[t]=fy[t]=fz[t]=0.0f; }\n    // loop over all j\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      float pjx = p[j].x;\n      float pjy = p[j].y;\n      float pjz = p[j].z;\n      for (int t = 0; t < imax - ii; ++t) {\n        int i = ii + t;\n        float dx = pjx - p[i].x;\n        float dy = pjy - p[i].y;\n        float dz = pjz - p[i].z;\n        float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n        float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n        float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n        fx[t] += dx * inv_dist3;\n        fy[t] += dy * inv_dist3;\n        fz[t] += dz * inv_dist3;\n      }\n    }\n    // update velocities\n    for (int t = 0; t < imax - ii; ++t) {\n      int i = ii + t;\n      p[i].vx += dt*fx[t];\n      p[i].vy += dt*fy[t];\n      p[i].vz += dt*fz[t];\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nThis uses variable-length arrays (VLA) fx[TI] where TI is constant, so fine. But if n < TI, imax-ii may be less than TI; we only use first few.\n\nPotential issue: OpenMP parallel for with schedule(static) will distribute iterations of ii loop among threads. Each iteration processes a tile independently. This preserves per-i summation order because each i belongs to exactly one tile and its forces are accumulated in j ascending within that tile's execution. Since tiles are independent, no cross-tile interference.\n\nWe also need to ensure the compiler doesn't reorder floating-point operations; but with -O3 it might use FMA or reassociate? Usually GCC respects strict FP unless -ffast-math. The baseline likely compiled without fast-math, so our code should produce same results if arithmetic order is preserved. Our inner loop adds contributions in j ascending exactly as original (for each i). So bitwise identical.\n\nWe also add restrict to pointer; that shouldn't change semantics.\n\nNow we need to output the full file content with this modification. Also maybe include <omp.h>? Not necessary for pragma, but if we want to use omp_get_num_threads etc no. We'll just leave as is.\n\nAlso note: The original code uses `1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist)` which is double division? Actually 1 is int, sqrt returns float (since sqrd_dist is float), so it's float division? In C, 1/sqrtf? It's integer divided by float -> float. We'll keep same.\n\nWe should also consider using `sqrtf` instead of `sqrt` for float; but that might change rounding? sqrt(float) returns double then converted to float? Actually sqrt takes double and returns double; if argument is float, it promotes to double, computes double sqrt, then assigned to float. That could differ from sqrtf which computes single precision directly. The baseline uses sqrt (double), so we must keep same to preserve exact output. So don't change.\n\nNow produce final file content.\n\nWe'll also maybe add a comment explaining transformation.\n\nLet's write the full src/nbody.c with modifications.\n\nAlso note: We should not modify other files. Only this one.\n\nSources_kept empty because integration_source_ids is empty.\n\nTransformations list: describe changes.\n\nHypothesis: Tiling over i and j improves cache reuse, combined with OpenMP parallelization over tiles, reduces memory traffic and exploits 4 cores while preserving exact floating-point summation order per body.\n\nNotes: mention that restrict enables compiler optimizations; tile size chosen to fit L1.\n\nLet's produce JSON."
